From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: selection for literacy

Paul BaClace:
> There's a strong correlation between availability of television and
> the lowering of birth rate.  

This is probably a "lucky penny".  There are millions of phenomena
out there and some of them will, by random chance, correlate closely
with birthrate.   Most phenomenon indicating a middle-class lifestyle
will correlate to lower birthrate.  I'll wager $100 that there's a 
(probably weak) correlation between increased TV viewing hours and 
increased birthrate in developed countries.

A reasonable social science hypothesis should show both good 
correlation _and_ a reasonable cause-and-effect scenario.  For
example, use of birth control correlated to fertility, and 
(via more subtle, but still reasonable cause-effect) education 
level tied to use of birth control (and therefore fertility).
It's improbable that TV viewing would increase birth control use,
unless it carries birth control ads, but weaker indirect causal chains
are there (eg those who can afford TVs can also afford birth control).

