Just as agriculture which used to be 90%+ of the economy is now <10%, 
physical goods are likely to drop to a similar percentage of the total 
economy.  Physical goods may already have fallen almost to this level in 
the US.  Most manufacturing and distribution has a very large service 
component (design, marketing, advertising, sales, management).  These 
services can be (and are being) unbundled and contracted out.  You are 
left with only a small physical component -- fulfillment.  

Even control of fulfillment can be denied them.  Imagine a world of 
lowered trade barriers and widespread catalog shopping.  A vastly expanded 
merger of today's consumer catalog shopping in the US and the growing use 
of EDI, Just In Time, and invoiceless purchasing by commercial firms.  
Orders are placed on the nets, random fulfillment companies satisfy those 
orders and are compensated.  Note that most of this transaction occurs on 
the nets and is untaxed.  The fulfillment companies are located in various 
jurisdictions and are paid an amount which may or may not be directly 
related to the total order price.  The taxes and controls on them will 
vary by location.  They may or may not pay taxes on some aspect of 
their operations. 

The movement of goods is already starting to overwhelm customs controls.  
It seems likely that the new free trade regions will eventually drop 
customs barriers within themselves.  You will then get an international 
version of the free for all in the US market.  Very hard to regulate.  
Remember that fulfillment companies will be able to change names (and even 
locations) with the speed of modern information processing.  (Just 
redirect the stream of goods flowing into and out of a fulfillment company 
to a new location.)

We will happily keep all the money (banking, insurance, finance), all the 
digital goods and services (telecommunications, entertainment, software, 
law, medicine, accountancy, sales, management, etc) and all those employed 
in these industries and the government can try and survive on the rest.

Problems for the government though.  Hard to maintain one's monopoly 
claims of power as one is controlling a smaller and smaller proportion of 
the economy and the lives of people.  They start to notice.

Duncan Frissell


