Date:     Wed, 22 Dec 93 17:43:44 GMT
From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Ithaca Hours

Ithaca Hours are a currency being used in Ithaca NY. They're
theoretically worth about an hour of labor, but people do
make adjustments for usually skilled or unskilled work.

I don't have the article handy, but I believe that the
H's are issued by one person. The important thing that
I don't remember is how you can get your initial batch
of hours from him--I know that it's 4 Hours, and I think
that it's for $10/Hour. He encourages people to trade
them locally and not to hoard them, but I assume that
both behaviors will occur if Hours really catch on.

In any case, there's a fair amout of local trade in
them--the craftspeople take them, as do some restaurants
and the local bank. The bank uses them to pay for printing.
(In case you were wondering, the bank keeps a *very* small
balance in Hours compared to its balance in dollars.)

While all transactions in Hours are supposed to be reported
to the IRS, the issuer makes it clear that this is the
responsibility of the people who use the hours.

Part of what I found interesting about the story is that
people make transactions in Hours that they wouldn't
make in dollars--craftspeople eating out more than they
otherwise would, for example.

People do worry about whether the Hours might lose their
value, but they don't seem to be worried enough to not
use them entirely.

I've wondered whether a similar currency could work in
science fiction fandom.

Nancy Lebovitz



