Duncan Frissell writes:

(quoting Nancy L., who was commenting on various interpretations of
others about my putative willingness to kill those I consider worthless...)

> N.>I think the suggestion is that, if you think that some people are
> N.>of no value whatsoever, then you might as well kill them whenever
> N.>it seems convenient or fun, and if you have scruples about killing
> N.>them, then you must think that they have some value. 
> 
> Perhaps Tim never finds killing convenient or fun.  Those (not Nancy) who 
> argue that if you don't think too much of the "great unwashed" you are as 
> likely as not to kill them are revealing the core of their own philosophy. 
>  Their collectivist attitude seems to be that we refrain from killing or 
> abusing "good people" (however defined) but it is open season on the rest 
> of the world.  Thus the collectivist's tendancy to kill large numbers of 
> people.  Libertarians can denigrate others without wanting to kill them.  
> It is only people of Janet Reno's philosophy who believe in killing those 
> who disagree with their political/social philosophy.  That is what the 
> word "totalitarian" means.  Tim is not a "totalitarian."

Precisely! I take the "nonaggression" belief of the libertarians as a
core belief, that is, I'm satisfied to let each person go to hell (or
heaven) in his own way, without interference, directly, from me. This
does not meen I won't "interfere" economically (by not trading with
them on terms they wish, for example), memetically, socially, etc.

(What I'm saying here, not especially clearly, is that I take a very
narrow view of "aggression" and "interference." If it involves holding
a gun to someone's head, literally or nearly so, then it's aggression.
Otherwise, it's not.)

Duncan's point about how Libertarians can denigrate others withought
wanting to kill them (or actually doing so, rather) is an important
one.

The dominant idea in America today is that one cannot criticize ohters
and others groups, that to do so is "discrimination" or "racism" and
is tantamount to physically assaulting them. Even on this List, we see
several people who argue that if I, for example, have no use for inner
city drug addicts and dropouts, that this means I must want to put
them into gas chambers and then cremate them.

"Tolerance" does not mean love, as so many people mistakenly think.
This is why I semi-jokingly call myself a "Neo-Calvinist." I can take
a stern view of various cultures and subcultures in the world today
without argung for their elimination. All I want is the natural right,
which is a Schelling point in many systems (I won't elaborate on this
now, except that I mean I'm not using "natural right" in any a priori,
religious, Randian sense...only in practical, PPL terms), to be left
alone and not to have to subsidize--or oppress--other lifestyles and
cultures.

I favor complete legalization of all drugs, not because I want to
take them myself (though I likely would experiment more if
pharmaceutical-quality drugs were cheaply available), and not because
I have compassion for inner city dwellers who are now the chief
recipients of assualts by the State in the War on (Some) Drugs.

In fact, if inner city dwellers overdose on legal drugs, I'll just
think "evolution in action" and will not "personalize" it. There are
billions of people on the planet, and thinking of each of them as if
they were one's brother or sister or child is, well, cognitively
dissonant.

Hence, I can be "uncaring" and "intolerant" withought desiring to send
storm troopers into neighborhoods I don't wish to visit to stop
activities I don't wish to subsidize, partake of, or otherwise have
any involvement with.

I consider this to be Libertarianism 101, and, frankly, I am surprised
that several people on this List have equated my selfishness and
Neo-Calvinism with totalitarian, fascist ideas and have repeatedly
attempted to smear me with allusions to death camps, crematoria, and
German accents.

--Tim May


